{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ps3NWK4khZ29YGEzEyLjc","type":"tool_result","content":"**1. Release, development, and hardware**\n- **Arcade:** North America, February 2003; Japan, April 1, 2003.\n- **PlayStation 2:** North America, October 21, 2003; Europe, October 31, 2003; Japan, November 20, 2003.\n- **Developers:** Namco and Nextech.\n- **Publishers:** Namco for arcade. For PS2, Namco Hometek in North America and Sony Computer Entertainment in Europe.\n- **Arcade hardware:** Namco System 246.\n\n**2. Gameplay**\n- The series' signature pedal system is used for hiding and advancing. The page doesn't explain how the pedal works in detail.\n- Weapon switching is described as a first for the series, but the page doesn't say how it's done (for example, whether pulling the trigger while hiding switches weapons).\n- **Four weapons:**\n  - A 9-round handgun, the only weapon with unlimited ammo.\n  - A fully automatic machine gun holding 200 rounds.\n  - A shotgun with 50 shells.\n  - A 5-round grenade launcher with splash damage.\n- **Ammo replenishment:** Shooting yellow-clad soldiers gives ammo for the non-handgun weapons.\n- **Crisis flash:** The system was refined so that \"life-threatening shots are brighter,\" which makes pedal-release warnings easier to notice.\n- **Not covered:** Time limit rules, how time is added, red-uniformed soldiers, and lives.\n\n**3. Two-player mode**\nTwo players can cooperate in a link play environment, as in Time Crisis II. The page gives no further details.\n\n**4. PS2-exclusive extras**\n- Alicia Winston is a playable character, having been a non-playable supporting character in the arcade version.\n- Some sections require her to use a sniper rifle against enemy snipers.\n- The Crisis Mission exercise menu can be reached through prolonged play.\n- The page doesn't mention a \"Rescue Mission.\"\n\n**5. Setting**\nSix years after Time Crisis II, the Mediterranean island of Astigos in Lukano is invaded by forces from the Zagorian Federation, led by General Giorgio Zott."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bbb0a1ea-12f9-46f9-9677-d2d253a967c8","uuid":"acc24e46-401b-4c90-b5f0-53cb7afa03df","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:57:44.420Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":151072,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**1. Release, development, and hardware**\n- **Arcade:** North America, February 2003; Japan, April 1, 2003.\n- **PlayStation 2:** North America, October 21, 2003; Europe, October 31, 2003; Japan, November 20, 2003.\n- **Developers:** Namco and Nextech.\n- **Publishers:** Namco for arcade. For PS2, Namco Hometek in North America and Sony Computer Entertainment in Europe.\n- **Arcade hardware:** Namco System 246.\n\n**2. Gameplay**\n- The series' signature pedal system is used for hiding and advancing. The page doesn't explain how the pedal works in detail.\n- Weapon switching is described as a first for the series, but the page doesn't say how it's done (for example, whether pulling the trigger while hiding switches weapons).\n- **Four weapons:**\n  - A 9-round handgun, the only weapon with unlimited ammo.\n  - A fully automatic machine gun holding 200 rounds.\n  - A shotgun with 50 shells.\n  - A 5-round grenade launcher with splash damage.\n- **Ammo replenishment:** Shooting yellow-clad soldiers gives ammo for the non-handgun weapons.\n- **Crisis flash:** The system was refined so that \"life-threatening shots are brighter,\" which makes pedal-release warnings easier to notice.\n- **Not covered:** Time limit rules, how time is added, red-uniformed soldiers, and lives.\n\n**3. Two-player mode**\nTwo players can cooperate in a link play environment, as in Time Crisis II. The page gives no further details.\n\n**4. PS2-exclusive extras**\n- Alicia Winston is a playable character, having been a non-playable supporting character in the arcade version.\n- Some sections require her to use a sniper rifle against enemy snipers.\n- The Crisis Mission exercise menu can be reached through prolonged play.\n- The page doesn't mention a \"Rescue Mission.\"\n\n**5. Setting**\nSix years after Time Crisis II, the Mediterranean island of Astigos in Lukano is invaded by forces from the Zagorian Federation, led by General Giorgio Zott.","durationMs":9960,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Crisis_3"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019ps3NWK4khZ29YGEzEyLjc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
